 17592186045585 true] [17592186129256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299673 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299674 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299675 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299578 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299580 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299719 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299720 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299677 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299735 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186129258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299736 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186134193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131167 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186134193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299502 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186134193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186134193 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation $R_{2}:=R_{2} - 3R_{3}$ is applied to a $3 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$. What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186134193 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186134193 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299699 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299702 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299703 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299858 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299867 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299868 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299869 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299870 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299936 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186147507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299937 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186175938 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299671 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186175969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299672 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186176085 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299918 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186176085 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299923 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186176086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299924 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186176086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299927 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299794 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299795 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299796 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299797 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299798 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299799 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299800 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299801 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186192811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299802 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299660 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299661 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216456 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216460 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298803 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298981 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299172 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299188 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299255 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299370 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299371 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217769 1071 "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{3}dx + xydy = 0, y > 0$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\inth(x)dy)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{y}$." 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217769 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3})=3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(3{y}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -(3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1/y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{y}^{-3})\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(-1/y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}\\,{e}^{-1/y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/y}({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216471 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299284 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217771 1071 "Given that $y_{0}(x) = e^{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$y^{′} = y^{2} - 2e^{x}y + e^{2x} + e^{x}$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217771 1071 "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2{e}^{x}y+{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217771 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2{e}^{x}y+{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=1,\\,b(x)=-2{e}^{x},\\,c(x)={e}^{2x}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+{e}^{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+{e}^{x}=(z+{e}^{x})^{2}-2{e}^{x}(z+{e}^{x})+{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & =({e}^{2x}+2{e}^{x}z+{z}^{2})-(2{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{x}z)+{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & ={z}^{2}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-{e}^{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216477 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299066 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217772 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}-y=0$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217772 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n1. $y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{3}$\n2. $yy^{′} - xy^{3} + 2xy^{2} = \\sin (x)y$\n3. $xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}} - y = 0$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217772 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{2}+2xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216483 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298756 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217773 1071 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186217773 1071 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy^{′} + e^{y^{′}}$." 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186217773 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′}{e}^{p} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+{e}^{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+{e}^{p}=0.$ Then, \n\n$$\nx=-{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{e}^{p}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{p})p+{e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-{e}^{p} \\\\ y=(1-p){e}^{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)\n$$\n\nSince $x=-{e}^{p}$ and ${e}^{p}>0,$ we must have $x<0.$ Thus, $p=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)$ is valid only for $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(1-p){e}^{p} \\\\  & =(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)){e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1),\\,x<0.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186233823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186232598 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186233823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298799 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186233827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186232610 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186233827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299264 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186233827 1071 "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 false] [17592186233827 1071 "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186233827 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$