ero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $v$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\nu⊥v\\,⟹\\,u\\cdot v=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot k+2\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4k-2-15 \\\\  & =4k-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 4k-17 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =17 \\\\ k & =\\frac{17}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298946 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299149 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299417 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1069 "q-316780" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1071 "Calculate ${A}^{-1},$ where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1072 #uuid "2eafafcb-8727-5ab6-b75d-030156359000" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299505 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(A)=6\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 9=-12\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-12}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1/12 & 1/6 \\\\ 3/4 & -1/2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1/12 & 1/6 \\\\ 3/4 & -1/2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1/12 & 1/6 \\\\ 3/4 & -1/2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299263 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299506 1069 "q-316781" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299506 1071 "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 5x-1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299506 1072 #uuid "95d2a0a0-79cd-50d5-95db-d0f4cfda76fd" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299506 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(2x)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot (5x-1) \\\\  & =-4x+4-20x \\\\  & =-24x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24x+4 & =0 \\\\ 24x & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{24} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299404 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299507 1069 "q-316782" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299507 1071 "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3x+2 & 2x-4\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299507 1072 #uuid "0e69fc32-4426-5cb8-9829-2fc1f8cd28fb" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299507 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =1\\cdot (2x-4)-2\\cdot (3x+2) \\\\  & =2x-4-6x-4 \\\\  & =-4x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-8 & =0 \\\\ -4x & =8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298977 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299508 1069 "q-316783" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299508 1071 "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}5x & -1 \\\\ 6x-8 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299508 1072 #uuid "a90dc1ce-cf3a-57f1-9a32-63fe824c4a01" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299508 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(5x)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (6x-8) \\\\  & =10x+6x-8 \\\\  & =16x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16x-8 & =0 \\\\ 16x & =8 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299071 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299509 1069 "q-316784" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299509 1071 "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -x+1 \\\\ 5 & 2x+7\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299509 1072 #uuid "1bc74342-0f37-5e04-9085-a552735d6bba" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299509 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(-3)\\cdot (2x+7)-5\\cdot (-x+1) \\\\  & =-6x-21+5x-5 \\\\  & =-x-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-26 & =0 \\\\ -x & =26 \\\\ x & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298817 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299510 1069 "q-316785" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299510 1071 "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 7x-3 & x+8\\end{aligned}]$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299510 1072 #uuid "6d52bc53-4740-5cbf-85a5-c4eda2c297f6" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299510 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (x+8)-(-2)\\cdot (7x-3) \\\\  & =4x+32+14x-6 \\\\  & =18x+26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18x+26 & =0 \\\\ 18x & =-26 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{13}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298802 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299368 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299511 1069 "q-316787" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299511 1071 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299511 1072 #uuid "5094386b-bf3b-5a24-bfbd-7ca6e6bf556a" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299511 1074 "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-3){e}^{x}+2x\\,{e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3){e}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ for all real $x,$ this reduces to\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-3>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x+3)(x-1)$ has roots at $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/9b/9b847390fb6de568144d309c45fb8c904475bdde79a46a90ec0fd4426a71b57b.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x+3)(x-1)$ is positive when $x<-3$ or $x>1.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x>1.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298698 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298768 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299512 1069 "q-316825" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299512 1071 "Let $B=[\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & 0 \\\\ 15 & -x-3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299512 1072 #uuid "113042b9-3913-565d-9ff6-26aa252c490f" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299512 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(2x+4)\\cdot (-x-3)-0\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-6x-4x-12 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-10x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-10x-12 & =0 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+5x+6) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-2.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298686 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298749 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299513 1069 "q-316828" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299513 1071 "Let $B=[\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & x-40 \\\\ 1 & -x\\end{aligned}]$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299513 1072 #uuid "5a81a924-c8d9-5da7-9d7c-338e9d3bb52d" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299513 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(2x+1)\\cdot (-x)-(x-40)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-x-x+40 \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}-2x+40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-2x+40 & =0 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+x-20) & =0 \\\\ -2(x-4)(x+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299124 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299409 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299514 1069 "q-316829" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299514 1071 "Let $B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -x+7 \\\\ x-5 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299514 1072 #uuid "e6886658-7f8f-54c1-b88e-4ea7a22c6205" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299514 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3\\cdot 0-(-x+7)\\cdot (x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x-7x+35 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =7,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=7.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298721 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299082 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299515 1069 "q-316847" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299515 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{5\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299515 1072 #uuid "bb2a4759-56e0-536a-934a-f3114c29c895" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299515 1074 "The denominator is $1+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-3\\text{i}}{1-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{i}-15{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{i}-15(-1)}{1-(-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{i}+15}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+5\\text{i}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15+5\\text{i}}{10}=(\\frac{3}{2})+(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298710 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298716 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299516 1069 "q-316848" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299516 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{7\\text{i}}{3-\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299516 1072 #uuid "a289044a-c00d-5726-8305-f04b6399fd17" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299516 1074 "The denominator is $3-\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $3+\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $3+\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\text{i}}{3-\\text{i}} & =\\frac{7\\text{i}}{3-\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{3+\\text{i}}{3+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\text{i}+7{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\text{i}+7(-1)}{9-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7+21\\text{i}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{-7+21\\text{i}}{10}=(-\\frac{7}{10})+(\\frac{21}{10})\\,\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299019 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299467 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299517 1069 "q-316850" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299517 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{8\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299517 1072 #uuid "8d2e7049-1420-58a2-b0dd-c8da4bdbd90b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299517 1074 "The denominator is $3-4\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $3+4\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $3+4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{8\\text{i}}{3-4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{3+4\\text{i}}{3+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\text{i}+32{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\text{i}+32(-1)}{9-(-16)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32+24\\text{i}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{-32+24\\text{i}}{25}=(-\\frac{32}{25})+(\\frac{24}{25})\\,\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298793 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299143 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299518 1069 "q-316851" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299518 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{5\\text{i}}{-2+3\\text{i}}=($\n {{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299518 1072 #uuid "85b1e292-ba3e-5912-bffe-69a8c8715a00" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299518 1074 "The denominator is $-2+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-2-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-2-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\text{i}}{-2+3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5\\text{i}}{-2+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-2-3\\text{i}}{-2-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\text{i}-15{\\text{i}}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\text{i}-15(-1)}{4-(-9)} \