f 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299532 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{-6-7\\text{i}}{2\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299532 1072 #uuid "6cdcc03d-9bc0-5aef-969c-114afb57f9f6" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299532 1074 "Here, the denominator is the imaginary number $2\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+2\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-2\\text{i},$ or simply $-2\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6-7\\text{i}}{2\\text{i}} & =\\frac{-6-7\\text{i}}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-2\\text{i}}{-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\text{i}+14{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-4{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\text{i}+14(-1)}{-4(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14+12\\text{i}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-14+12\\text{i}}{4} & =-\\frac{14}{4}+\\frac{12}{4}\\text{i} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{7}{2})+(3)\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298688 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298998 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299533 1069 "q-316891" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299533 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{-5-9\\text{i}}{10\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299533 1072 #uuid "7822b8b5-e9d2-5645-891c-e3cc1d2ee54a" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299533 1074 "Here, the denominator is the imaginary number $10\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+10\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-10\\text{i},$ or simply $-10\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-10\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5-9\\text{i}}{10\\text{i}} & =\\frac{-5-9\\text{i}}{10\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-10\\text{i}}{-10\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{50\\text{i}+90{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-100{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{50\\text{i}+90(-1)}{-100(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-90+50\\text{i}}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-90+50\\text{i}}{100} & =-\\frac{90}{100}+\\frac{50}{100}\\text{i} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{10})+(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298682 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298730 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299534 1069 "q-316893" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299534 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{7}{3-4\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299534 1072 #uuid "a0df5ab2-e3a9-51a6-90ae-3e454d5e41c0" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299534 1074 "The denominator is $3-4\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $3+4\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $3+4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3-4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{7}{3-4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{3+4\\text{i}}{3+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21+28\\text{i}}{9+12\\text{i}-12\\text{i}-16{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21+28\\text{i}}{9-16(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{21+28\\text{i}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{25}+\\frac{28}{25}\\text{i}=(\\frac{21}{25})+(\\frac{28}{25})\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299125 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299147 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299535 1069 "q-316896" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299535 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{7}{-3-2\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299535 1072 #uuid "e670c179-3ce4-5739-9685-d9ed11fc889b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299535 1074 "The denominator is $-3-2\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-3+2\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-3+2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{-3-2\\text{i}} & =\\frac{7}{-3-2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-3+2\\text{i}}{-3+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(-3+2\\text{i})}{(-3)^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-21+14\\text{i}}{9-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-21+14\\text{i}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-21+14\\text{i}}{13}=(-\\frac{21}{13})+(\\frac{14}{13})\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298689 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299426 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299536 1069 "q-316904" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299536 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{5+2\\text{i}}{-2-3\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299536 1072 #uuid "1e0f218a-eb72-503e-b6ad-3bd1a87517fb" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299536 1074 "The denominator is $-2-3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-2+3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-2+3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5+2\\text{i}}{-2-3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5+2\\text{i}}{-2-3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-2+3\\text{i}}{-2+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5+2\\text{i})(-2+3\\text{i})}{(-2)^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10+15\\text{i}-4\\text{i}+6{\\text{i}}^{2}}{4-(-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10+11\\text{i}+6(-1)}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{-16+11\\text{i}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-16+11\\text{i}}{13}=(-\\frac{16}{13})+(\\frac{11}{13})\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299412 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299537 1069 "q-316928" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299537 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299537 1072 #uuid "aa7637fa-17fe-5b0f-ac5e-e3d4fe42a92c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299537 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{100}=10,\\,b=\\sqrt{25}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{10}{5}x \\\\  & =\\pm 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299133 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299538 1069 "q-316934" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299538 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $8{x}^{2}-50{y}^{2}=400$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299538 1072 #uuid "c443605f-7b41-5190-85eb-bb42dfbb4297" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299538 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $400$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}-50{y}^{2} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{8{x}^{2}}{400}-\\frac{50{y}^{2}}{400} & =\\frac{400}{400} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{50}$ and $b=\\sqrt{8}.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{\\sqrt{50}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{5\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{5}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299181 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299539 1069 "q-316942" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299539 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=8$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299539 1072 #uuid "4a97fc3c-d20b-51ae-b431-f48c8240cb2d" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299539 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $8$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{8}$ and $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{8}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299452 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299540 1069 "q-316951" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299540 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}=36$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299540 1072 #uuid "f53f6741-18c2-588b-99cb-7ed60af32ad6" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299540 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $36$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{36}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{9}=3$ and $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299216 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299541 1069 "q-316956" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299541 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $18{y}^{2}-50{x}^{2}=900$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299541 1072 #uuid "9543b0ad-7ba3-57c4-a1e6-3f27f7375f33" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299541 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $900$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{y}^{2}-50{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ \\frac{18{y}^{2}}{900}-\\frac{50{x}^{2}}{900} & =\\frac{900}{900} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and $b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{50}}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298926 13194139533412 17592186045585 tr