2186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299577 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. The velocity of the particle equals $(-4i+9j)\\,\\text{ft/s}$ both when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ and $t=7\\,\\text{s}.$ What is the acceleration of the particle?\n\n$a=($\n{{field-1}}$\\,i$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,)\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299577 1072 #uuid "f1f21675-899a-596e-94a0-b4d75a7e1672" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299577 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-4i+9j,\\,v=-4i+9j,\\,t=7\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ -4i+9j & =(-4i+9j)+a(7) \\\\ 0 & =7a \\\\ a & =(0\\,i+0\\,j)\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298976 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299407 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299578 1069 "q-317185" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299578 1071 "The velocity of a particle is $(i-7j)\\,\\text{km/h}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{h}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i+4j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=3\\,\\text{h}.$\n\n$v=($\n{{field-1}}$i$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$j\\,)\\,\\text{km/h}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299578 1072 #uuid "deb9c00a-fef3-5025-8bf9-5d22b0b6fd07" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299578 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=i-7j,\\,a=3i+4j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=3\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(i-7j)+(3i+4j)(3) \\\\  & =(i-7j)+(9i+12j) \\\\  & =(10i+5j)\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298806 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298875 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299168 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299219 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1069 "q-317187" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1071 "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=-4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$ Fill the table with appropriate sign of $f(x,y)$ as shown below.\n\n| $y>0$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1072 #uuid "d89c9a62-af8b-597a-a7b7-d5170a201bc4" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299579 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5fd85410238231152b6f905921f594af15293d6efc54b0b1ce558131a05bf6ee.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/e9/e95107f16eb80a06cd172c30ec78992838f974a91f9f2fa17b7c8ac77125c438.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-4{x}^{2}$ | ${y}^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/ff/ff354ce9051d24e6f0adc4e0def143de86b36418b0b9846fdac1b4a63ca630f3.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299010 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299213 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299580 1069 "q-317195" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299580 1071 "The velocity of a particle is $(-3i+5j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(2i-j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v=($\n{{field-1}}$i$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$j\\,)\\,\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299580 1072 #uuid "1d209858-bc50-5d67-b79d-da96ab5fa189" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299580 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=-3i+5j,\\,a=2i-j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(-3i+5j)+(2i-j)(4) \\\\  & =(-3i+5j)+(8i-4j) \\\\  & =(5i+1j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298846 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299251 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299350 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299430 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299581 1069 "q-317208" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299581 1070