d $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the unknown elements of $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,5=2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 5 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,7=4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298880 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298916 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299659 1069 "q-317848" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299659 1071 "Determine $AB$ given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-6$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $11$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299659 1072 #uuid "630b6f29-5fc9-5ea7-9ec7-b34bacfad40e" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299659 1074 "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and $B$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $A$ and $4$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the unknown elements of $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,-4=1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -2\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,-2=4\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 11 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298734 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299660 1069 "q-317872" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299660 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-2}?$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299660 1072 #uuid "8b94a25c-e733-5f52-b78f-f15218b66d53" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299660 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/6d/6dd511da4a6bab6e37383620852446da06e84207517efc23165544f823ed330f.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $2$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/4d/4d60dc4fe52cd717370ae1eeb28dd35b0c3c8e4a4e69ff088b16e56c03be5735.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-2}.$ The point $(2,1)$ moves to $(2,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4b042273bfb3a6640e57c75eff3058dd177bc2d75dfdf312160a4a6c9a9de53.png)" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298873 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299661 1069 "q-317883" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299661 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=-{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-3}?$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299661 1072 #uuid "ca2a4879-7d4c-5a1b-978e-4d781c194dca" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299661 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/2a/2a5b83a177193d9a6be738cd521410fa3369afaec3f88f121d42616c51313696.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $3$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-3}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/90/905c163e5836693180cd5ccee9af63f875433c243ef630126762df3fe74f98d5.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-3}.$ The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/03/03b657beccb9c7afd2c652f44ba70cf15c186463715acafe35df10e650831aec.png)" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299235 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299238 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299279 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299374 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1069 "q-317900" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1071 "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $A\\,x=b.$ Here, $A$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $x$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $b$ is the column vector containing the constants. What are the missing parts of the derivation method shown below to find the unique solution of the system?\n\n$$\nA\\,x={{field-1}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$\n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1072 #uuid "cce553b5-2cba-5d0f-a25a-d7275ae86d94" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299662 1074 "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x=b$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299281 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299663 1069 "q-317910" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299663 1071 "If $a=⟨5,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299663 1072 #uuid "18bcf01e-256b-5339-97ad-00f972b0c662" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299663 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =10+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298690 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299664 1069 "q-317911" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299664 1071 "If $u=⟨8,-2⟩$ and $v=⟨3,6⟩,$ then $u\\cdot v=${{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299664 1072 #uuid "02b21351-2b17-5c15-a1fb-09ee1d2960d3" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299664 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24-12 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298861 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298898 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299151 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1069 "q-317924" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $u$ onto $v.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,u=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1072 #uuid "47bb7ff9-8595-5c3c-8469-6716c5ae622e" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299665 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,u & =\\frac{u\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}_{x}{v}_{x}+{u}_{y}{v}_{y}+{u}_{z}{v}_{z}}{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 1+(1)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}v \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{5}v \\\\  & =⟨\\,0,0,0\\,⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299237 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299666 1069 "q-317932" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299666 1071 "If $c=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ $d=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ and $c⊥d,$ then $k=${{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299666 1072 #uuid "6840a760-5144-5da8-bd56-8faedecfd9c9" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299666 1074 "We know that $c⊥d$ ($c$ and $d$ are perpendicular) if and only if $c\\cdot d=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-3)+2k\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3k+4k+8 \\\\  & =k+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298719 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299202 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299301 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1069 "q-317938" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1071 "Let $r=\\,i+3j+4k$ and $s=3i-j.$ Find the vector projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{s}\\,r=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1072 #uuid "d68b8f9a-f16c-5356-aa77-f2b44657e973" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299667 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{s}\\,r & =\\frac{r\\cdot s}{s\\cdot s}s \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}s \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot 3+(3)\\cdot (-1)+(4)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}s \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}s \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299349 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299668 1069 "q-317943" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299668 1071 "Let $a=t\\,i+4j-k$ and $b=2i-j.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $t.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299668 1072 #uuid "0770665b-e1ee-5c0b-9b14-a8d432ab894e" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299668 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular.\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =t\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2t-4+0 \\\\  & =2t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 2t-4 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =4 \\\\ t & =2 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298869 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299669 1069 "q-317944" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299669 1071 "Let $a=t\\,i-4j+k$ and $b=4i+j.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $t.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299669 1072 #uuid "2940b787-7de0-58e6-a5d9-3477f08d2d69" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299669 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular.\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =t\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4t-4+0 \\\\  & =4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 4t-4 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299175 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299670 1069 "q-317945" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299670 1071 "Let $a=⟨2{x}^{2},-4⟩$ and $b=⟨8,4⟩.$ If $a⊥b$ and $x>0,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299670 1072 #uuid "d22ee02b-54e6-511a-8cc5-59280c888981" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299670 1074 "We know that $a⊥b$ ($a$ and $b$ are perpendicular) if and only if $a\\cdot b=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot 8+(-4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $x>0.$ Therefore, $x=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298823 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299671 1069 "q-317978" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299671 1071 "Given that $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a solution to the equation $4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299671 1072 #uuid "ea9202ac-8801-52e2-86b4-596e2b355837" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299671 1074 "Let $f(x)=4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+100x+25.$ Since $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+\\frac{1}{4})$ using synthetic division.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $1$ | $100$ | $25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $4$ | $0$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}+100).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $4{x}^{2}+100=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(5\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298876 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299173 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299672 1069 "q-317986" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299672 1071 "Determine all the solutions to ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25=0$ given that $x=5\\text{i}$ is a solution. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=\\pm $\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299672 1072 #uuid "37fee550-d43c-5be5-8e4d-f60181e15823" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299672 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{5\\text{i}}=-5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i}))$ is a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(5\\text{i})(-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+25\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+25x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}\\,-25 \\\\  & \\,\\,{\\,-{x}^{2}\\,\\,-25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+25)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm \\,5\\text{i}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298672 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299382 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299673 1069 "q-318172" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299673 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $-8i\\,\\text{km/h},$ and when $t=6\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $(4i-18j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle.\n\n$a=($\n{{field-1}}$\\,i$\n$-$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299673 1072 #uuid "71b3a37d-cf06-58d8-aa97-cc67cc3e8878" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299673 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-8i,\\,v=4i-18j,\\,t=6,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 4i-18j & =-8i+a(6) \\\\ 12i-18j & =6a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{6}(12i-18j) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299185 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299187 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299674 1069 "q-318173" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299674 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $(3i-8j)\\,\\text{km/h},$ and when $t=4\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $(15i+8j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle.\n\n$a=($\n{{field-1}}$\\,i$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299674 1072 #uuid "f8d8e8ef-cc1c-587f-a5bd-d76c4b12453c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299674 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=3i-8j,\\,v=15i+8j,\\,t=4,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 15i+8j & =(3i-8j)+a(4) \\\\ 12i+16j & =4a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}(12i+16j) \\\\  & =(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299029 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299096 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299675 1069 "q-318175" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299675 1071 "A particle moves with constant acceleration. The velocity of the particle equals $(9i-4j)\\,\\text{km/h}$ both when $t=0\\,\\text{h}$ and $t=5\\,\\text{h}.$ What is the acceleration of the particle?\n\n$a=($\n{{field-1}}$\\,i$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299675 1072 #uuid "b2412070-98e5-5d9c-a01e-de215c58ccb2" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299675 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=9i-4j,\\,v=9i-4j,\\,t=5\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 9i-4j & =(9i-4j)+a(5) \\\\ 0 & =5a \\\\ a & =(0\\,i+0\\,j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299451 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299676 1069 "q-318182" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299676 1071 "A particle moves with a constant velocity of $u=-20i\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ Find the speed of the particle.\n\n$|u|=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{ft/s}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299676 1072 #uuid "c48b0c50-88cb-5988-b997-303f6437d6ad" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299676 1074 "The speed of a particle equals the magnitude of its velocity.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u| & =|-20i| \\\\  & =|-20| \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfeet per second." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299203 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299677 1069 "q-318188" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299677 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(-2i+j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(6i-8j)\\,\\text{km/h}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{h}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(-8i-j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{h}$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299677 1072 #uuid "5ed019b7-25c8-59bd-bf51-0a8c294fa696" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299677 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=6i-8j,\\,v=-8i-j,\\,a=-2i+j,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ -8i-j & =(6i-8j)+(-2i+j)t \\\\ -14i+7j & =(-2i+j)t \\\\ -14i+7j & =-2ti+tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14=-2t \\\\ 7=t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=7\\,\\text{h}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299678 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299245 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299678 1069 "q-318223" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299678 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299678 1071 "![](images/55/551c38c3bedd56d8fc1c4fef9f1fcc15b9fbf6fa83af81253b7a4d2296aaeb38.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299678 1072 #uuid "f931cf9f-2dac-54ec-8d65-3efbaae6ccc0" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299678 1074 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-1]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1]$, the three line segments for $x\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6+3+7 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299179 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299679 1069 "q-318225" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299679 1071 "![](images/95/95e601203c55e93922554e4eb56a9742f4a84466ac387e3358484baaf9287e08.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299679 1072 #uuid "c349db8f-5b7a-5939-87f5-078e4758e4b6" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299679 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,2]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [2,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299152 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299680 1069 "q-31823" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299680 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{x}^{2}-2x)$ at $x=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299680 1072 #uuid "dae8ef74-e145-5880-a0a9-2063f507b28b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299680 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{x}^{2}-2x)] \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+(3{x}^{2}-2x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1+9{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}\\cdot (6x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{36x-12}{9{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{36(1)-12}{9({1}^{4})-12({1}^{3})+4({1}^{2})+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298705 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299085 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299274 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299283 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1069 "q-318306" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1071 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],$\nthen\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1072 #uuid "1c732374-c38e-5da0-a638-380740d98619" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299681 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3 & -3\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 0\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3 & 0\\cdot (-5)+4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+6 & 15+4 \\\\ 0+12 & 0+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 19 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298980 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298984 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299032 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299035 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1069 "q-318308" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1071 "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],$\nthen\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1072 #uuid "5f44856d-8dd6-5629-abca-06def3aba707" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299682 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 5 & 3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 5 & (-1)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9+(-10) & 6+2 \\\\ 3+20 & -2+(-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-19 & 8 \\\\ 23 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298753 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298770 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299031 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299406 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1069 "q-318311" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1071 "If $C=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$\nthen\n\n| $CD=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1072 #uuid "9aeb9fb1-11fc-52ad-8f0e-fdbd7fcf849e" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299683 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2