139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299701 1071 "Compute the following:\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299701 1072 #uuid "2383f09c-491e-571a-94ba-6ef74710119b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299701 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot 3 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6\\cdot (-1) & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2\\cdot 4 \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 & + & (-3)\\cdot (-1) & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,7\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1\\cdot 3 & + & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,8\\cdot (-1) & + & (-2)\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & + & (-6) & + & 8 \\\\ 15 & + & 3 & + & 28 \\\\ 3 & + & (-8) & + & (-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 46 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299311 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299702 1069 "q-318629" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299702 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299702 1072 #uuid "70dbdf12-3bc6-5349-9efa-62768bd9b3d9" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299702 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}+y,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+2) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is not exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3xy,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy) \\\\  & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-4x)=4x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=6y-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299387 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299703 1069 "q-318630" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299703 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299703 1072 #uuid "861223b8-6640-50cd-ba1a-46920dc4bc74" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299703 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy+5,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+5) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+7) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{2}+x,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-2x)=2x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y-3x) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation III is not exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298900 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299261 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299704 1069 "q-318640" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299704 1071 "Compute the following:\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299704 1072 #uuid "f86a8d09-8955-5065-97fa-d72868178f2c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299704 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 5+11\\cdot (-1) \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot 5+2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298816 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299009 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299705 1069 "q-318644" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299705 1071 "Compute the following:\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299705 1072 #uuid "a0f60f35-e715-5fe3-a08e-1735aa11be89" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299705 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-7)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-34 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-34 \\\\ 5\\cdot 4+8\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-34 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-34 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299204 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299706 1069 "q-31871" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299706 1071 "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299706 1072 #uuid "d5898f79-8d87-5e96-a0ad-da8516834a84" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299706 1074 "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298726 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299707 1069 "q-318964" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299707 1071 "Suppose $|\\,k\\,|=2\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $k$ and $l$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $k$ onto $l.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{l}\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299707 1072 #uuid "f95bba43-9c68-572a-b72d-21ed2ae6f555" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299707 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{l}\\,k & =|\\,k\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299447 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299708 1069 "q-318971" 13194139533412 175