 $0$ | $3$ |  |\n| **C** | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298851 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299751 1069 "q-319350" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299751 1071 "Differentiate ${e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)).$\n\n$({e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299751 1072 #uuid "c9a55990-04ba-5f4c-bd0b-a335f0aeb15a" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299751 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=2+sin⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}sec⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2+sin⁡(x))+({e}^{x})(cos⁡(x)) \\\\  & =(2+sin⁡(x)){e}^{x}+{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299041 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299752 1069 "q-319357" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299752 1071 "Differentiate $2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x).$\n\n$(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299752 1072 #uuid "dca45644-472a-517e-a9dd-987f8f5907bc" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299752 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡(x)+2{x}^{2}\\cdot (cos⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(4x)(cos⁡(x))+(2{x}^{2})(-sin⁡(x)) \\\\  & =4xcos⁡(x)-2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299054 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299243 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299384 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1069 "q-319360" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1071 "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-5.$\n\n|  |  | $-17$ | {{field-1}} | $-1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $12$ | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |\n|  |  | $-3$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1072 #uuid "0876fd99-09ab-5402-abb3-c07e19b050b7" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299753 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-17 & -12 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 12 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298947 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299754 1069 "q-319369" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299754 1071 "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-2,$ and ${x}_{4}=5$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$ Determine the **expanded form** of $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,40$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299754 1072 #uuid "2a2a380e-e34f-5fdc-b2f2-0da0d4c05ded" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299754 1074 "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-2,$ and ${x}_{4}=5,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =1(x-(-2))(x-(-2))(x-(-2))(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x+2)(x+2)(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x+2)(x+2)(x-5) & =({x}^{2}+4x+4)({x}^{2}-3x-10) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-40x+4{x}^{2}-12x-40 \\\\  & ={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}-52x-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}-52x-40.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298670 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298907 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299484 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1069 "q-319370" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1071 "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-4.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | $4$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $7$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1072 #uuid "228d219f-a988-51e3-add6-8253de28fd6f" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299755 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298949 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299068 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299239 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1069 "q-319372" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1071 "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=7.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $-1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\,$ |  | $-11$ | {{field-2}} | $3$ |  |\n|  |  | $-4$ | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1072 #uuid "74394102-8729-5a30-8d7e-62d8f0117047" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299756 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 11 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -11 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299166 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299757 1069 "q-319377" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299757 1071 "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-1,$ and ${x}_{4}=3$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $2.$ Determine the **expanded form** of $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,6$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299757 1072 #uuid "71134303-8f46-5305-9d47-063807ada843" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299757 1074 "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-1,$ and ${x}_{4}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =2(x-(-1))(x-(-1))(x-(-1))(x-3) \\\\  & =2(x+1)(x+1)(x+1)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+1)(x+1)(x+1)(x-3) & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)({x}^{2}-2x-3) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-6x+{x}^{2}-2x-3) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-0{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-8x-3) \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}-16x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=2{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}-16x-6.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299444 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299758 1069 "q-319385" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299758 1071 "Differentiate $\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x).$\n\n$(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′}=\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299758 1072 #uuid "9df2a3ce-ad57-5588-83d2-42b69ca3314d" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299758 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡(x)+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (tan⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}(tan⁡(x))+(\\sqrt{x})({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298818 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299055 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299156 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299169 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1069 "q-319395" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1071 "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ {{field-2}} |  | $-1$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1072 #uuid "ca1a8117-1d26-5acd-beac-beb6e4c1cfa3" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299759 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(2-3)-0(\\,-2-(-3)\\,)+(-3+3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299099 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299760 1069 "q-319409" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299760 1071 "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299760 1072 #uuid "e7e72b45-0695-5b36-9476-7542514f8aaf" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299760 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-3-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298712 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298889 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299052 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299761 1069 "q-319412" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 