 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(2-(-3))+2(4-0)+0(6-0) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}^{t}={c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1+4) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299500 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299764 1069 "q-319420" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299764 1071 "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{31}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299764 1072 #uuid "d6120ca6-9e08-5dd7-8756-664c0b14c937" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299764 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(2+1)-0\\,(0-1)+2\\,(-0-2) \\\\  & =3-0-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}^{t}={c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298771 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299765 1069 "q-319425" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299765 1071 "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}tan⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299765 1072 #uuid "6ccc1718-8889-5c4c-8f82-a3c2895dd7ec" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299765 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{(tan⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}ln⁡(4))tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298727 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299397 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299476 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1069 "q-319429" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1071 "![](images/76/76cff940632cac02300b538a4ef063cfe3e8a699b2d406c998725a6c6fa1eee6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed lines between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Fill in the missing elements of the table.\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |\n| **B** | $1$ | {{field-2}} | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1072 #uuid "b4dd82a5-6212-589b-be41-2adb16a361ad" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299766 1074 "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are $3$ arrows starting at Station A and ending at Station C. So, there are $3$ routes from A to C.\n\n- There are $2$ arrows starting at Station B and ending at Station C. So, there are $2$ routes from B to C.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station B. So, there is $1$ route from C to B.\n\nTherefore, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| **B** | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298738 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299767 1069 "q-319434" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299767 1071 "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $det(A)=1-2k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299767 1072 #uuid "b74699a3-68fd-5996-962f-7d078766e370" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299767 1074 "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=1-2k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot k-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-2k)}\\cdot (-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3k & =2k-1 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298924 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299768 1069 "q-319437" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299768 1071 "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299768 1072 #uuid "d4e42e14-a47f-5067-a3e2-ccca26a8935c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299768 1074 "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-1\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1+2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{k-1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k-1 & =-2 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298810 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299769 1069 "q-319438" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299769 1071 "Write ${x}^{2}+36$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{2}+36=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299769 1072 #uuid "d25f385f-873f-55c8-8593-4bb48f19b21f" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299769 1074 "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+36 & ={x}^{2}-(-36) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(36{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+6i)(x-6i)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299107 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299770 1069 "q-319442" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299770 1071 "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=-2k-3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299770 1072 #uuid "f7cdf079-0dbd-550b-985e-14ed1ec133fd" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299770 1074 "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-2k-3\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1+2) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2k-3)}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 12 & =2k+3 \\\\ 2k & =9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299458 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299771 1069 "q-319443" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299771 1071 "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299771 1072 #uuid "24df41a5-9c85-5f3d-b3b0-277ec8e095a9" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299771 1074 "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-2\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}|\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(k-2)}\\cdot (k+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(k+2) & =-(k-2) \\\\ 2k+4 & =-k+2 \\\\ 3k & =-2 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299190 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299772 1069 "q-31946" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299772 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299772 1072 #uuid "9a2668c7-61c5-574f-ad15-7bac1011df33" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299772 1074 "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299303 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299773 1069 "q-319471" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299773 1071 "Write ${x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299773 1072 #uuid "2cc5cfc5-d134-5dda-a506-6d9144ee1bb3" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299773 1074 "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${x}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25 & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot ({x}^{2})+{5}^{2} \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+5)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5 & ={x}^{2}-(-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(5{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25 & ={({x}^{2}+5)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2}{(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299402 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299774 1069 "q-319492" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299774 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-5+3\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299774 1072 #uuid "a5f26b04-c224-5e5b-b7ec-b6e734fe3cb5" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299774 1073 true 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299774 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/21/217fc7ba4c6a6c18d0be184a9d5cc7b0a682ed2bd0da6e284955040643bc2e03.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$\n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =0.540\\,419…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -0.540\\,419…≈2.60,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299493 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299775 1069 "q-319499" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299775 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-11+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299775 1072 #uuid "19bf53c8-97d2-5dba-bc67-23661ef3ef49" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299775 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d3/d3b5558c0268843618c52c588ffcf1c8012189041b17d52fadd6fe74c3526700.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{8}{11} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{8}{11}) \\\\  & =0.628\\text{ }796\\text{ }…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=\\pi -0.628\\text{ }796\\text{ }…≈2.51,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299139 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299776 1069 "q-319506" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299776 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-3+3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299776 1072 #uuid "d850cf07-df72-5541-8c7d-7d2b4b77160b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299776 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d00bb449e3525bed0543e690ef41895da5ad9bd28632f127aa7d8e5876ce7deb.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299448 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299777 1069 "q-319507" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299777 1071 "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-5+5\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299777 1072 #uuid "11e7ecac-aef1-58c7-ae41-03384a6b83c3" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299777 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/f4/f466a110eda3b07b50d988de6e46a541ad85d7ff64f43a6f056ea216752fa2d9.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{5} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298785 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299778 1069 "q-31951" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299778 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=2arcsec(6x-1).$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299778 1072 #uuid "1b0ce731-e82d-557a-b699-13d5ea673ea4" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299778 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $arcsec$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsecx)=\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2arcsec(6x-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsec(6x-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{(6x-1)^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{{(6x-1)}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-12x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{4(9{x}^{2}-3x)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{|6x-1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-3x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299779 1068 #edb/ref 1759218629