},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k-4}{2k+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k+2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =2 \\\\ k+2 & =2k+2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298854 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299819 1069 "q-319949" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299819 1071 "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-2,$ ${x}_{3}=-4,$ and ${x}_{4}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $9.$ Determine the **expanded form** of $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,576$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299819 1072 #uuid "fe096008-e96b-5eb5-b514-d5a3a3f47c60" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299819 1074 "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-2,$ ${x}_{3}=-4,$ and ${x}_{4}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=9,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =9(x-(-2))(x-(-2))(x-(-4))(x-4) \\\\  & =9(x+2)(x+2)(x+4)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x+2)(x+2)(x+4)(x-4) & =9({x}^{2}+4x+4)({x}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =9({x}^{4}-16{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-64x+4{x}^{2}-64) \\\\  & =9({x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-64x-64) \\\\  & =9{x}^{4}+36{x}^{3}-108{x}^{2}-576x-576.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=9{x}^{4}+36{x}^{3}-108{x}^{2}-576x-576.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298765 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298913 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299353 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1069 "q-320021" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1072 #uuid "34cd5f5a-f82e-5ae5-899e-5e1bd32cf8b9" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299820 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $(AB)$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)\\,C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 & 48 & -60\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298783 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299198 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299821 1069 "q-320026" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299821 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299821 1072 #uuid "7d60a4e0-4fdb-56f1-b62d-65e7bbd5fd84" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299821 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-58 & 136\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298733 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299011 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299126 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299497 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1069 "q-320045" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1071 "Calculate $3A$, where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $3A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1072 #uuid "ca7933b5-7926-5b69-927f-7d4e31439a2a" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299822 1074 "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =3[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & 3\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298813 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298839 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299823 1069 "q-320072" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299823 1071 "![](images/1c/1c4fdc9fd5139049eca98953578210be6ea17fcfb4588f4a855087347a603f3f.png)\n\nExpress the complex number represented in the Argand diagram above in the form $a+bi,$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$i$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299823 1072 #uuid "66d20cbd-a952-5372-83e8-88eccad29bdf" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299823 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(-4,6).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-4,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=6.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-4+6\\,i.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299159 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299824 1069 "q-320074" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299824 1071 "![](images/fb/fb8d76e5fb2e83676fbab4178b523bf8cbff076490f12fa8be17c37ce06e1820.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-