2186298865 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299830 1069 "q-320122" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299830 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i?$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\overset{―}{z}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299830 1072 #uuid "fd7e9641-0012-5d34-88e4-626c0af69b6b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299830 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i,$ the imaginary part is $-\\sqrt{2}\\,i$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $\\sqrt{2}\\,i.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=\\sqrt{2}\\,i.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298937 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299831 1069 "q-320124" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299831 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-\\frac{5}{4}\\,i?$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\overset{―}{z}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299831 1072 #uuid "1800f0e6-73ef-55e8-8f9f-1d0fb84b72c8" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299831 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-\\frac{5}{4}\\,i,$ the imaginary part is $-\\frac{5}{4}\\,i$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $\\frac{5}{4}\\,i.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=\\frac{5}{4}\\,i.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298986 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299832 1069 "q-320134" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299832 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-\\sqrt{7}?$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\overset{―}{z}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299832 1072 #uuid "919d2784-1462-578b-a289-17ea9c4d1b86" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299832 1074 "The number $z=-\\sqrt{7}$ is a real number, and the complex conjugate of any real number is just the number itself.\n\nSo here, the complex conjugate is $\\overset{―}{z}=z=-\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299036 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299833 1069 "q-320138" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299833 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-2\\sqrt{2}?$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\overset{―}{z}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299833 1072 #uuid "f1a58455-9e23-5d03-9836-a67488f0af0a" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299833 1074 "The number $z=-2\\sqrt{2}$ is a real number, and the complex conjugate of any real number is just the number itself.\n\nSo here, the complex conjugate is $\\overset{―}{z}=z=-2\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299070 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299109 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299834 1069 "q-320150" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299834 1071 "![](images/05/05366af0819a90f962643e0cc64977d716cc345c76dd5d6810528633ed5edd65.png)\n\nExpress the complex number represented in the Argand diagram above in the form $bi+a,$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$i\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299834 1072 #uuid "01a11fb4-0b50-551b-a7ba-508372cf5e71" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299834 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(2,6).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=2,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=6.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=2+6i.$ Finally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2+6i \\\\  & =6\\,i+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299242 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299835 1069 "q-320185" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299835 1071 "![](images/6a/6a39af16798593716291ab84bfff4460279420f493bd10959782312187f0bf44.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $2.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,72$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299835 1072 #uuid "d2d05cab-afc8-555d-96ed-d65f47562369" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299835 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1,2}=\\pm \\text{i}$ and ${x}_{3,4}=\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=2.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(x-(-\\text{i}))(x-\\text{i})(x-(-6\\text{i}))(x-6\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2(x+\\text{i})(x-\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i})(x-6\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+\\text{i})(x-\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i})(x-6\\text{i}) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+(\\text{i}-\\text{i})x-{\\text{i}}^{2})({x}^{2}+(6\\text{i}-6\\text{i})x-36{\\text{i}}^{2}) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+1)({x}^{2}+36) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}+36{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+36) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}+37{x}^{2}+36) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+74{x}^{2}+72 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+74{x}^{2}+72.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299324 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299836 1069 "q-320235" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299836 1071 "![](images/4f/4f960f00fbcbde79dee3603112ed08e8c34cf87b3ccbe781ee5acbf61a4482fd.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $2.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,52$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299836 1072 #uuid "40827e6d-ccf8-5737-bd80-bc43201366b8" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299836 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1,2}=-2\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and ${x}_{3,4}=1\\pm \\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=2.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(x-(-2+3\\text{i}))(x-(-2-3\\text{i}))(x-(1+\\text{i}))(x-(1-\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-(-2+3\\text{i}))(x-(-2-3\\text{i}))(x-(1+\\text{i}))(x-(1-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+4x+4+9)({x}^{2}-2x+1+1) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+4x+13)({x}^{2}-2x+2) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+8x+13{x}^{2}-26x+26) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-18x+26) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-36x+52 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-36x+52.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298945 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299837 1069 "q-32027" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299837 1071 "Given the matrix\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find ${B}^{-1}$ by performing row operations." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299837 1072 #uuid "8762936c-5e8e-5984-8bdd-fb153d2e6740" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299837 1074 "We consider the augmented matrix $[B\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{B}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299174 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299838 1069 "q-320276" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299838 1071 "![](images/58/58136b4140d772431a823b66b03ebfa95bd51816e4df9be7f2401c29c97a5495.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $3.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,36$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299838 1072 #uuid "7837fbb5-9b5f-5218-b805-da7b7a49139e" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299838 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-3,$ ${x}_{2}=2,$ and ${x}_{3,4}=-1\\pm \\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=3.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =3(x-(-3))(x-2)(x-(-1+\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(x+3)(x-2)(x-(-1+\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)(x-2)(x-(-1+\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-2x+3x-6)({x}^{2}-x(-1-\\text{i})-x(-1+\\text{i})+(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+x-6)\\,({x}^{2}+2x+2) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+2x-6{x}^{2}-12x-12) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x-12) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{4}+9{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-30x-36 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+9{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-30x-36.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299093 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299839 1069 "q-32028" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299839 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-7y=0 \\\\ -3x+6y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299839 1072 #uuid "ea9e053d-85c3-58a4-8c39-2db422348c5d" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299839 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299229 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299840 1069 "q-32029" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299840 1071 "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find ${A}^{-1}$ by performing row operations." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299840 1072 #uuid "12e685a6-44e2-5006-b584-d214b4233509" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299840 1074 "We consider the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  \\\\  & =[I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298778 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299841 1069 "q-320292" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299841 1071 "![](images/4e/4eafeae1c77e3e75cb2b281181346eeb7e96ca5e1bd15ec11d722109469557a1.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $3.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,24$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299841 1072 #uuid "25d4cc1b-6157-5691-8648-fe2233c0942f" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299841 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1,2}=\\pm 2\\text{i}$  and ${x}_{3,4}=1\\pm \\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=3.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =3(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i}))(x-(1+\\text{i}))(x-(1-\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})(x-(1+\\text{i}))(x-(1-\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})(x-(1+\\text{i}))(x-(1-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+2\\text{i}x-2\\text{i}x-(2\\text{i})^{2})({x}^{2}-x(1-\\text{i})-x(1+\\text{i})+(1+\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-2x+2) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}-8x+8) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-8x+8) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-24x+24 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-24x+24\\,.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299018 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299167 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299842 1069 "q-320315" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299842 1071 "If $P$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix and $Q$ is a $3\\times 7$ matrix, then the dimensions of $PQ$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299842 1072 #uuid "39c32253-c4be-58a1-b0ce-c95df6ac7d80" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299842 1074 "There are $3$ columns in $P$ and $3$ rows in $Q.$ Since the number of columns in $P$ equals the number of rows in $Q,$ the matrices $P$ and $Q$ are conformable for multiplication, and $PQ$ is a $4\\times 7$ matrix:\n\n$$\n{P}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{Q}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 7}={PQ}_{4\\times 7}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299217 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299843 1069 "q-32033" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299843 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y=0 \\\\ -3x+6y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299843 1072 #uuid "3b398440-0508-554f-852f-fbbc5195e967" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299843 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 6-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299314 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299395 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299844 1069 "q-320344" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299844 1071 "If $X$ is a $5\\times 7$ matrix and $Y$ is a $10\\times 5$ matrix, then the dimensions of $YX$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299844 1072 #uuid "394112ca-a03d-53c1-90e3-844365de46b0" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299844 1074 "There are $5$ columns in $Y$ and $5$ rows in $X.$ Since the number of columns in $Y$ equals the number of rows in $X,$ the matrices $Y$ and $X$ are conformable for multiplication, and $YX$ is a $10\\times 7$ matrix:\n\n$$\n{Y}_{10\\times \\boxed{\\text{5}}}{\\times }_{✓}{X}_{\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\times 7}={YX}_{10\\times 7}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298849 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299223 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299845 1069 "q-320351" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299845 1071 "Let\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\nThen, the dimensions of $CD$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299845 1072 #uuid "cbad9be4-81c3-5d76-a5dd-452794f75baa" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299845 1074 "We notice that $C$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix and $D$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $C$ and $1$ row in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $C$ equals the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $CD$ has dimensions $4\\times 3:$\n\n$$\n{C}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\times }_{✓}{D}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3}={CD}_{4\\times 3}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298657 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299846 1069 "q-32037" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299846 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299846 1072 #uuid "76e5a507-45db-59c1-877b-e0a6e215a18d" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299846 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298961 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299344 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299847 1069 "q-320371" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299847 1071 "Let\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$F=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\nThen, the dimensions of $FE$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299847 1072 #uuid "cef63f89-6766-5942-a14a-b8ab45d7b4ca" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299847 1074 "We notice that $E$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix and $F$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $F$ and $4$ rows in $E.$ Since the number of columns in $F$ equals the number of rows in $E,$ the matrices $F$ and $E$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $FE$ has dimensions $2\\times 2:$\n\n$$\n{F}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{E}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={FE}_{2\\times 2}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298985 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299848 1069 "q-32038" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299848 1071 "Let  $E=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299848 1072 #uuid "890b7708-4d96-51de-8064-24b0a048633e" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299848 1074 "The matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the first row of the identity matrix  by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:=3{R}_{1}$ to $A.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298828 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299048 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299849 1069 "q-320389" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299849 1071 "Let\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$F=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Then, the dimensions of $EF$ are{{field-1}}$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299849 1072 #uuid "283919b2-9c02-5df9-b56d-dbcf89785d7c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299849 1074 "We notice that $E$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix and $F$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. \n\nThere are $2$ columns in $E$ and $2$ rows in $F.$ Since the number of columns in $E$ equals the number of rows in $F,$ the matrices $E$ and $F$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $EF$ has dimensions $3\\times 1:$ \n\n$$\n{E}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{F}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 1}={EF}_{3\\times 1}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299459 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299850 1069 "q-320392" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299850 1071 "![](images/68/6881c7b52de83103dcfc483ec25455781a4d399653e2369d28f6924327f8ee56.png)\n\nDetermine the area $A$ of the parallelogram in the figure.\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299850 1072 #uuid "362d0901-58c5-537a-b4a8-7e15773992b7" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299850 1074 "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨-3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨1,3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9-2 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-11|=11\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298920 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299851 1069 "q-320394" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299851 1071 "![](images/f6/f63cf622acc15681e3815118bbd2a187ea6039a548d4be1773634c0dc450c343.png)\n\nDetermine the area $A$ of the parallelogram in the figure.\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299851 1072 #uuid "2106b5f7-d155-5686-8911-c293b62f546c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299851 1074 "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨4,-1⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨-2,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-14|=14\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298787 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299852 1069 "q-320401" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299852 1071 "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299852 1072 #uuid "0ae47b66-7d53-5c4c-a6bb-e60ff971339c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299852 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8-15 \\\\  & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-23\\,|=23\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299063 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299853 1069 "q-320402" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299853 1071 "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299853 1072 #uuid "b877ff9e-20e8-50e1-8344-e018774f6173" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299853 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =15-2 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,13\\,|=13\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299201 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299854 1069 "q-320404" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299854 1071 "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299854 1072 #uuid "586460f4-83f8-52e0-990d-cdd1c6e95132" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299854 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-33\\,|=33\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299171 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299855 1069 "q-320412" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299855 1071 "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨2,7⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨-3,1⟩.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299855 1072 #uuid "3e9f3e3d-c238-5325-aee8-71b571be0f5d" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299855 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-(-21) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299275 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299856 1069 "q-320413" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299856 1071 "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨-4,-1⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨6,2⟩.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299856 1072 #uuid "7553ad76-d7b1-5eca-9463-9df9742eb383" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299856 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8-(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-2\\,|=2\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299194 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299857 1069 "q-320414" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299857 1071 "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨5,-3⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨-2,-4⟩.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299857 1072 #uuid "216d9367-5418-5e10-afd0-533eb5320c38" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299857 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-20-6 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-26\\,|=26\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298895 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298968 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298992 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299141 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299184 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299369 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299468 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1069 "q-320450" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1071 "Determine whether the following differential equation is exact:\n\n$$\n(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nA differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it satisfies the condition\n\n$$\n\\,{{field-1}},\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0$, we have  \n\n$$\nP=\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\n$$\nQ=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\,{{field-6}},\n$$\n\n the equation {{field-7}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1072 #uuid "a4505d16-2cca-5cfc-8b1c-8092ab2f2f00" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299858 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0$, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}+3y.\n$$\n\nNow, compute the partial derivatives carefully:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\,(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{xy}+x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\,(x{e}^{xy}+3y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nso the exactness condition fails. Therefore, the given differential equation $\\text{is not exact}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299415 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299859 1069 "q-320536" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299859 1071 "If $kB=[\\begin{aligned}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhere $B=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299859 1072 #uuid "c737dee9-8d9f-5c6a-907a-8f6ef5631464" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299859 1074 "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k[\\begin{aligned}11 & 12 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11k & 12k \\\\ 4k & -8k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}11k & 12k \\\\ -4k & -8k\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}33 & 36 \\\\ -12 & -24\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the bottom-left elements, $-4k$ and $-12.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4k & =-12 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299300 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299860 1069 "q-320550" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299860 1071 "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-5⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,6⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299860 1072 #uuid "53380aeb-e3ec-5770-a193-a3eec01995c1" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299860 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6-a\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =9+5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =-9 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299861 1069 "q-320551" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299861 1071 "If $kW=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhere $W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299861 1072 #uuid "c212ad13-93dd-5e76-9471-11a5cb69eaba" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299861 1074 "To express $kW,$ we multiply each entry of $W$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kW & =k[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kW$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4k & -3k \\\\ 2k & -2k & 5k\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -15 \\\\ 10 & -10 & 25\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the  leftmost elements of the third row, $2k$ and $10.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =10 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299023 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299862 1069 "q-320553" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299862 1071 "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨-\\frac{4}{3},7⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,-9⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299862 1072 #uuid "6597267d-a0da-5d5e-b28e-9dd4c1c2688b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299862 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot (-9)-a\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-7a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-7a & =0 \\\\ 7a & =12 \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299097 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299863 1069 "q-320556" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299863 1071 "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨3\\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{5}⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,\\,10⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299863 1072 #uuid "2e91baa4-e2fe-58ac-ba3a-3b023f074435" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299863 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 10-a\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ a\\sqrt{5} & =-30\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ a & =-6\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298777 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299864 1069 "q-320557" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299864 1071 "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299864 1072 #uuid "53138b4d-25df-5dbe-9072-3708ca850fe6" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299864 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 6-a\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{2}-3a & =0 \\\\ -3a & =-12\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298792 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299865 1069 "q-320558" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299865 1071 "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299865 1072 #uuid "fb3235d6-3da0-50ae-8be8-4a92d3030a44" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299865 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{7})(-10)-a\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{7}-5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\sqrt{7}-5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =10\\sqrt{7} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299088 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299866 1069 "q-320563" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299866 1071 "If $kB=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhere $B=[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299866 1072 #uuid "ef847762-e432-58b6-805a-1384db601a50" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299866 1074 "To express $kB,$ we multiply each entry of $B$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kB & =k[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kB$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}24k & -12k & 6k\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the rightmost elements, $6k$ and $2.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298663 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299867 1069 "q-320578" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299867 1071 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}{\\,13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299867 1072 #uuid "9285c033-5bae-5080-b27a-b8c40b5b8da1" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299867 1074 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}{13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{4}{y}^{3}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\,(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\,4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\,(x\\,(xy)^{3}-13y) \\\\  & =\\,4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =22x+\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}+f(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{4}{y}^{3}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=22x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+22x=c.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299292 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299868 1069 "q-320585" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299868 1071 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299868 1072 #uuid "5bdd84df-d1f6-5328-b5af-cb71bdcbb4a7" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299868 1074 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by$(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+3x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$  when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot 1)-(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =-1-1 \\\\ c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298736 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299869 1069 "q-320586" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299869 1071 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{\\,4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299869 1072 #uuid "f17832a3-73ac-531a-b06a-a29c9fd5eb32" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299869 1074 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=-2$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot (-2))-2(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =0-8 \\\\ c & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299129 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299870 1069 "q-320589" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299870 1071 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{\\,4y-x{e}^{xy}\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=4.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299870 1072 #uuid "f2cb23bd-6a0e-55a8-8740-0231e5c31fd5" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299870 1074 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{4y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-5,\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-5) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-4y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=4$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x & =c \\\\ {e}^{0\\cdot 4}-2(4)^{2}-5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =1-32 \\\\ c & =-31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299135 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299871 1069 "q-320620" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299871 1070 #edb/ref 