412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299876 1071 "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299876 1072 #uuid "0a983de3-38d8-5868-9695-96338b07b5e3" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299876 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299453 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299877 1069 "q-320661" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299877 1071 "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299877 1072 #uuid "fc2c455c-830c-5664-8a21-f28282f7535e" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299877 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298962 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299878 1069 "q-320667" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299878 1071 "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299878 1072 #uuid "82b0ad06-80da-5022-a24d-7d7a5827c2fe" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299878 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298808 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299247 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299339 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299341 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1069 "q-320671" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N$, where\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$N=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $\\frac{2}{3}M+2N=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-12$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1072 #uuid "c7edc5be-5384-5064-85e3-0d10b5e614fd" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299879 1074 "We multiply each entry of $M$ by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and each entry of $N$ by $2,$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}M+2N & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 15 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 15 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-12) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2) & 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+6 & 0-2 & 10+0 \\\\ -8-4 & 4+1 & 0-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -2 & 10 \\\\ -12 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299094 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299880 1069 "q-320673" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299880 1071 "The equation of the hyperbola $14{x}^{2}-21{y}^{2}=126$ in standard form is{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299880 1072 #uuid "f2deaa7c-e065-523b-b096-d4b288306c46" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299880 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $126$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{2}-21{y}^{2} & =126 \\\\ \\frac{14{x}^{2}}{126}-\\frac{21{y}^{2}}{126} & =\\frac{126}{126} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298656 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299881 1069 "q-320675" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299881 1071 "The equation of the hyperbola $45{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}=135$ in standard form is{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299881 1072 #uuid "54b5fd86-3d2b-5b48-84c2-d1b54392706a" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299881 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $135$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2} & =135 \\\\ \\frac{45{x}^{2}}{135}-\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{135} & =\\frac{135}{135} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{27} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298883 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299882 1069 "q-320676" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299882 1071 "The equation of the hyperbola $7{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}=84$ in standard form is{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299882 1072 #uuid "74b8bddf-e834-5082-af8b-2f3e7f728eec" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299882 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $84$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2} & =84 \\\\ \\frac{7{y}^{2}}{84}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{84} & =\\frac{84}{84} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{28} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298993 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299883 1069 "q-320683" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299883 1071 "The equation of the hyperbola $20{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}=120$ in standard form is{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299883 1072 #uuid "d5f0adc5-3cf5-5cbd-8841-7d33e75b58fa" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299883 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $120$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2} & =120 \\\\ \\frac{20{y}^{2}}{120}-\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{120} & =\\frac{120}{120} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299073 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299884 1069 "q-320685" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299884 1071 "The equation of the hyperbola $12{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}=72$ in standard form is{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299884 1072 #uuid "6d289418-3c5e-5788-9cb2-618ff6500fe6" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299884 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $72$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2} & =72 \\\\ \\frac{12{y}^{2}}{72}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{72} & =\\frac{72}{72} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299189 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299199 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299360 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1069 "q-320686" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1071 "Find the points where the curve $x=4t,\\,y=\\frac{32}{t}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0.$\n\nThe intersection points are $(-16,$\n{{field-1}}$)$ and $(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1072 #uuid "effc7d02-1d17-59c9-91fd-2df011435a17" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299885 1074 "To find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4t)-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2t-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2{t}^{2}-32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-32 & =0 \\\\ 2({t}^{2}-16) & =0 \\\\ 2(t+4)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y.$ \n\n- We start with $t=-4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(-4) \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the first intersection point are $(-16,-8).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(4) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the second intersection point are $(16,8).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=4t,\\,y=\\frac{32}{t}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0$ at the points $(-16,-8)$ and $(16,8).$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299423 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299886 1069 "q-320695" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299886 1071 "Write ${x}^{2}+49$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${x}^{2}+49=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299886 1072 #uuid "48a7ede8-0285-5672-b20b-caa4e4429a1b" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299886 1074 "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+49 & ={x}^{2}-(-49) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(49{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(7i)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+7i)(x-7i)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299209 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299399 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299887 1069 "q-320696" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299887 1071 "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $2\\sqrt{3}\\,y=x$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299887 1072 #uuid "de2328b7-9e15-5845-9b2b-25744372387c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299887 1074 "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299051 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299294 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299888 1069 "q-320705" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299888 1071 "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299888 1072 #uuid "4cb0fec8-288b-5688-a0ea-decdd504c292" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299888 1074 "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298942 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299450 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299889 1069 "q-320707" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299889 1071 "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $3y+x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299889 1072 #uuid "123c9826-f851-5809-a519-215f25691f30" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299889 1074 "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298739 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299042 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299372 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1069 "q-320718" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1071 "![](images/c9/c90af67d867e33c847f66721c1abbc6768fbc34e7f39648fbc2e4f486a87ca3f.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What are the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $k?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,k=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1072 #uuid "a1e8bac4-2103-5a0e-a08e-e356983c6103" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299890 1074 "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=2.5,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$ This means that $k=2.5$ and $b=-2,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-(-2)}+2.5=\\frac{a}{x+2}+2.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(-5,3.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x+2}+2.5 \\\\ 3.5 & =\\frac{a}{-5+2}+2.5 \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{a}{(-3)} \\\\ a & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298936 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299891 1069 "q-320721" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299891 1071 "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299891 1072 #uuid "9e965292-76e8-5eea-83c9-7985bc2f61f3" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299891 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299091 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299892 1069 "q-320730" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299892 1071 "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299892 1072 #uuid "ef8bf712-474f-56cc-83a0-2804b7e92977" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299892 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={-30}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298789 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299893 1069 "q-320731" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299893 1071 "Write $49{r}^{2}+9$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$49{r}^{2}+9=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299893 1072 #uuid "c5eb0c8c-40dc-5ae8-bbeb-207361ae72fc" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299893 1074 "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{r}^{2}+9 & =49{r}^{2}-(-9) \\\\  & =49{r}^{2}-(9{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(7r)^{2}-(3i)^{2} \\\\  & =(7r+3i)(7r-3i)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298922 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299894 1069 "q-320739" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299894 1071 "Write ${m}^{2}+25{n}^{2}$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${m}^{2}+25{n}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299894 1072 #uuid "1cef1c54-5acf-58ad-938e-af3ca7588f9c" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299894 1074 "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25{n}^{2} & ={m}^{2}-(-25{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={m}^{2}-(25{i}^{2}{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={m}^{2}-(5in)^{2} \\\\  & =(m+5in)(m-5in)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299210 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299895 1069 "q-320744" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299895 1071 "Write ${r}^{6}+4$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n${r}^{6}+4=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299895 1072 #uuid "003c125b-7f65-54b5-8e0f-48cc74be74b7" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299895 1074 "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{r}^{6}+4={({r}^{3})}^{2}+{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{6}+4 & ={r}^{6}-(-4) \\\\  & ={r}^{6}-(4{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({r}^{3})}^{2}-{(2i)}^{2} \\\\  & =({r}^{3}+2i)({r}^{3}-2i)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298711 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299896 1069 "q-320771" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299896 1071 "Write $16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10}$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299896 1072 #uuid "fea0b4d4-4a5f-50b9-8907-2393dcb577fa" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299896 1074 "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10}={(4{p}^{6})}^{2}+{({m}^{5})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{p}^{12}+{m}^{10} & =16{p}^{12}-(-{m}^{10}) \\\\  & =16{p}^{12}-({i}^{2}{m}^{10}) \\\\  & ={(4{p}^{6})}^{2}-{(i{m}^{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{6}+i{m}^{5})(4{p}^{6}-i{m}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186298763 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299897 1069 "q-320776" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299897 1071 "Write $18{c}^{2}+50$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$18{c}^{2}+50=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299897 1072 #uuid "dfe4b36a-178d-55f0-8168-e9287a1b9293" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299897 1074 "First, let's factor out $2$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n18{c}^{2}+50=2\\,(9{c}^{2}+25).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(9{c}^{2}+25).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{c}^{2}+25 & =9{c}^{2}-(-25) \\\\  & =9{c}^{2}-(25{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3c)^{2}-(5i)^{2} \\\\  & =(3c+5i)(3c-5i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\,(9{c}^{2}+25)=2(3c+5i)(3c-5i).\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299244 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299898 1069 "q-320780" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139