6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3\\,(4{t}^{2}+36)=-3(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299136 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299900 1069 "q-320785" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299900 1071 "Write $-18{s}^{2}-32$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$-18{s}^{2}-32=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299900 1072 #uuid "d389e8d1-439c-513a-a8cb-4b08ae0a6cba" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299900 1074 "First, let's factor out $-2$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n-18{s}^{2}-32=-2\\,(9{s}^{2}+16).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(9{s}^{2}+16).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{s}^{2}+16 & =9{s}^{2}-(-16) \\\\  & =9{s}^{2}-(16{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3s)^{2}-(4i)^{2} \\\\  & =(3s+4i)(3s-4i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2\\,(9{s}^{2}+16)=-2(3s+4i)(3s-4i).\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186299308 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299901 1069 "q-320788" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299901 1071 "Write $49{t}^{9}+36{t}^{5}$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$49{t}^{9}+36{t}^{5}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299901 1072 #uuid "009839f0-9375-5f7f-a859-9b3fc9dede61" 13194139533412 17592186045585 true] [17592186299901 1074 "First, let's factor out $